WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.891
Oscar Serna is speaking in a brand new language. 

00:00:04.891 --> 00:00:09.744
Oscar: I'm really stressed out!

00:00:09.744 --> 00:00:13.018
Oscar is both deaf and blind, or, "DeafBlind."

00:00:13.018 --> 00:00:18.869
He works at Gallaudet University, on a project tracking the evolution of a language for those who can’t see or hear. 

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:22.514
This new language is called pro-tactile ASL. 

00:00:22.514 --> 00:00:26.926
The ASL stands for American Sign Language, which uses visual signs for words and phrases.  

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:34.100
Pro-tactile ASL communicates entirely through touch.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.840
For example, here’s a sentence in ASL:

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.441
Clifton in ASL: I cut down a tree.

00:00:39.441 --> 00:00:42.761
Here’s how Oscar would say the same sentence in pro-tactile ASL:

00:00:42.761 --> 00:00:47.846
Oscar in PTASL: I cut down a tree.

00:00:47.846 --> 00:00:53.047
Historically, DeafBlind people communicated through American Sign Language, Braille, and fingerspelling, 

00:00:53.047 --> 00:00:56.391
where each letter of each word is signed into a person’s hand. 

00:00:56.391 --> 00:01:00.686
Hellen Keller, maybe the world’s most famous DeafBlind person, used fingerspelling. 

00:01:00.686 --> 00:01:05.121
But those are limiting, especially when DeafBlind people want to talk to each other.

00:01:05.121 --> 00:01:11.599
Pro-tactile ASL emerged in the early 2000s, as once-isolated DeafBlind people began to form communities.

00:01:11.599 --> 00:01:18.356
DeafBlind people have been adapting American Sign Language and adding gestures for things many languages don’t have words for. 

00:01:18.356 --> 00:01:22.094
For example, this tap on the hand is like nodding.

00:01:22.094 --> 00:01:26.366
The language has been evolving ever since. 

00:01:26.366 --> 00:01:31.456
Clifton: “Now what’s new in pro-tactile is that we’re seeing things that were 

00:01:31.456 --> 00:01:37.214
used in visual sign language be transitioned from the use of space to the use of the perceiver’s body.”

00:01:37.214 --> 00:01:44.064
Traditional theories of language defined it as something seen or heard. 

00:01:44.064 --> 00:01:48.360
But Pro-tactile ASL proves that language can also be communicated through touch.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:53.143
And for the people speaking it, it allows for a life with richer communication. 

00:01:53.143 --> 00:02:01.339
Oscar: "Since I became pro-tactile, all of the channels have opened up; information flows freely."

00:02:01.339 --> 00:02:05.542
It’s like going from dial-up to broadband.

